  "body": "Found while working #785.\n\nThe machine check (`crates/app/src/machine/mod.rs`, the pose branch of the limit judge) judges a tilted move's **letters** from `cl::axis_to_angles`. It does no IK, by design.\n\nA 3+2 tilt stores canonical letters (A in -90..90), so the face underneath a bar arrives as **A0 B180**. On a machine whose only rotary is an **A about X** (the new `Tormach 770MX + microARC 4`), the check refuses it: \"commands B 180.000 deg; ... states no B axis\". Yet that A reaches the pose as A180, and the Tormach post (#785) already reads the pose through the tool axis and posts it as `G0 A180`.\n\nSo today a 4th-axis job can't cut the face at 180 degrees. It has to use the opposite pair A-90 / A90 (the #785 test part does).\n\n**Done means:** on a machine stating one rotary about X, the check accepts any tool axis with no X component and judges its angle about X against that rotary's travel. Every other pose is still refused by name. Pinned by a test that posts an A0 B180 Slot on the 770MX + microARC 4.\n\nEffort: high (a collision/limit check, under the safety floor).\n",
